doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21444597"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Conefia/SAFE-CARE-Bench"
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21444597"
abstract: >
  Patient-facing healthcare AI agents now talk with people across many turns, and each new
  version should pass a release gate, the go-live check that asks whether a change made the
  agent safer without losing accuracy. Single-turn medical benchmarks were not designed to
  answer that question, because a conversational agent fails across turns: risk builds while
  each reply looks acceptable, and an escalation to human care can arrive one turn too late.
  SAFE-CARE Bench is a benchmark specification and evaluation protocol designed for that
  decision. Its configurations declare which capabilities are active, not how to build them,
  so a team can reproduce the protocol on its own runtime. To trace a change to its cause,
  SAFE-CARE Bench runs a controlled ablation. Five agent variants, tiers A0 to A4, add one
  capability bundle at a time: retrieval with enforced citations, a safety guardrail,
  personalization from memory, and agency (tools, profile write-back and closed-loop actions).
  All five are scored over the same simulated conversations with synthetic personas, with the
  model, temperature and seed held fixed, so the design attributes the change between adjacent
  tiers to the bundle added. An LLM judge scores ten dimensions, seven on a 1-to-5 scale and
  three as pass or fail, and judges safety and escalation both on a single turn and across
  turns. A missed escalation fails a conversation whatever its other scores. The worked
  example, tiers A0 to A2, shows why the instrument must be checked before its verdict is
  trusted. We expected the safety guardrail to raise judged safety. Instead, adding it lowered
  judged medical accuracy by 0.157 on a 0-to-1 scale, lower in six of nine scenarios and
  higher in none, while the 95% interval for its safety change included zero. Auditing the
  instrument then showed that the scenario file decides, before any agent runs, which turns
  each judge reads. As a result, 33 of 75 turns (44%) went unscored at every tier, and 12 of
  the 36 turns on which the guardrail intervened were read by no safety or escalation judge.
  With nine scenarios in scope and a judge not yet calibrated against clinicians, these
  results are exploratory, and every score should be read as indicative, not validated.
keywords:
  - healthcare AI
  - AI agents
  - evaluation
  - benchmark
  - ablation study
  - LLM-as-judge
  - guardrails
  - safety
  - multi-turn dialogue
  - retrieval-augmented generation
references:
